  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Annotated Printed Chart",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Annotated Printed Chart\"**\n\nThe system is the category standard played straight: white cards on a cool grey ground, slate ink, one blue accent, Inter with tabular figures, a 1px hairline border and a 10px radius. Nothing about the shell is themed. All the character is in the Smith chart itself, which reproduces the printed paper chart students use in class: three tiers of grid that fill in as you zoom, four labelled outer rings, and curved captions in capitals. The interface's job is to put the answer, the chart and the textbook steps side by side as equals and connect them.\n\nDensity is calm-technical. It aims at the craft of Desmos/GeoGebra (a chart that feels like a precise instrument), Smith V4.1/ADS (the notation and scales of the real chart) and Notion/Linear (quiet chrome, exact spacing, restrained states). Colour carries meaning, never decoration: blue is solution 1 and the primary action, orange is solution 2, red is the target, and dashed grey is construction. The lettered point badges A–E are the thread that ties the chart, the legend and the steps together, and they look the same in all three places and on paper.\n\nThe same page is also a printed A4 handout. Print is a first-class output, not an afterthought, so every rule has to hold up in black and white with no interaction.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Three columns: inputs, then the chart as hero, then answers above steps. Answers stay above the fold on ~700px-tall laptops.",
      "A single blue accent, plus solution, target and construction colours that only appear in their roles.",
      "Inter throughout with tabular figures (`tnum`) and the `cv11` alternate.",
      "Hairline borders, a 10px card radius, one soft ambient shadow.",
      "A chart that copies the printed Smith chart, scaled so strokes and labels keep their screen size at any zoom.",
      "Step ↔ point linking through identical lettered badges."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Colour-Is-Role Rule",
        "body": "Each chromatic colour has exactly one meaning: blue means solution 1 or the primary action, orange means solution 2, red means a target or forbidden zone, and dashed grey means construction. Never use them as decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Swap Rule",
        "body": "Solution colour comes only from `--sol`, which is switched by `.app[data-sol]`. A component that shows \"the current solution\" reads `var(--sol)` and never hard-codes blue or orange.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Follow-the-Device Rule",
        "body": "Light or dark follows the device's `prefers-color-scheme` (requested by the user on 2026-10-21); there is no in-app toggle.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Every number is set in tabular figures. Subscripts (`<sub>` at 0.7–0.72em) follow the course notation: z<sub>L</sub>, d<sub>max</sub>, Γ<sub>L</sub>.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
